 buying into  what he has to say  Oh stoicism is not hot anymore  Epicureanism I'll read Lucretius no that  can't be our approach to things we have  to be responsible he thinks if we're  really going to be taking on  intellectual spiritual religious truths  we have to be responsible as individuals  taking them on now he also tells us that  and this is a little bit more tricky to  make sense of he says that the truth has  to be communicated and received with God  as what he calls the middle term so what  does he what does he mean by that he  says the truth can either be  communicated nor received without being  as it were before the eyes of God does  that mean that you have to be in a  church no but that means that you have  to be holding yourself accountable to  something there's something bigger than  yourself to something transcendent  something that thinks that truth really  matters and further than further going  beyond that Kierkegaard uses the  Christian notion of God  not only being true not only being  interested in truth but being truth  itself here to talk about that relation  so he says that the truth can neither it  neither be communicated nor received  without God's help nor without God being  involved as the middle term since he is  the truth so when were appropriating  truth were in a way appropriating God  were also being helped by the truth to  understand truth and you might say well  that's a really weird way to talk  however if you look through the history  of Christian thinkers some of whom  aren't you know identifiable as  existentialist and aren't talking about  the crowd or anything like that you will  find this as a motif st. Anselm speaks  in this way and he probably got it from  Saint Augustine who also talks in this  way during you know dialogues the  teacher will say to the students great  example of somebody who is communicating  the truth as a single individual to a  single individual and we're looking at  as a third single individual t